\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The controlled comparison on 437 rows: one encoder, one corpus, only the input differs. AUPRC is the per-seed mean $\pm $ standard deviation over three seeds; P/R/F1 are three-checkpoint ensembles at block-held-out thresholds. The teacher's score is $P(\textsc  {yes})$ from its first answer token; its P/R/F1 use the same block-held-out thresholds, and the triple question is its labelling prompt, asked in both argument orders and maxed like the trained triple arm (prompts in Appendix~\ref  {app:prompts}). Against the sentence-level model, the pair-conditioned one fixes 75 items and breaks 22 (McNemar $p = 1.3\times 10^{-7}$) and the triple-conditioned one fixes 74 and breaks 32 ($p = 6.8\times 10^{-5}$); triple against pair, 20 fixed and 31 broken ($p = 0.16$). Per-seed values are in Appendix~\ref  {app:seeds}.\relax }}{4}{table.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:main}{{1}{4}{The controlled comparison on 437 rows: one encoder, one corpus, only the input differs. AUPRC is the per-seed mean $\pm $ standard deviation over three seeds; P/R/F1 are three-checkpoint ensembles at block-held-out thresholds. The teacher's score is $P(\textsc {yes})$ from its first answer token; its P/R/F1 use the same block-held-out thresholds, and the triple question is its labelling prompt, asked in both argument orders and maxed like the trained triple arm (prompts in Appendix~\ref {app:prompts}). Against the sentence-level model, the pair-conditioned one fixes 75 items and breaks 22 (McNemar $p = 1.3\times 10^{-7}$) and the triple-conditioned one fixes 74 and breaks 32 ($p = 6.8\times 10^{-5}$); triple against pair, 20 fixed and 31 broken ($p = 0.16$). Per-seed values are in Appendix~\ref {app:seeds}.\relax }{table.caption.10}{}}
